	<h2>16. Geographies of Empire</h2>
	<div class="content-content">






<figure class="wp-block-image alignwide size-large"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="512" src="https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/School-Begins1-1024x512.jpg" alt="A political cartoon depicts Uncle Sam as a schoolmaster reprimanding dark-skinned children, representing the United States’s newly acquired territories, as well-behaved light skin children, representing U.S. states, read quietly behind them." class="wp-image-1297" srcset="https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/School-Begins1-1024x512.jpg 1024w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/School-Begins1-300x150.jpg 300w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/School-Begins1-768x384.jpg 768w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/School-Begins1-1536x768.jpg 1536w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/School-Begins1.jpg 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">“School Begins,” Puck, January 25, 1899.</figcaption></figure>







